const faqs = [
    {
        question: "What is microservices monitoring?",
        answer:
            "Microservices monitoring is measuring every service in a distributed system the same way, and following each request through all the services and queues it passes. It combines per-service metrics such as request rate, error rate and latency, distributed traces that join the spans of one request under one trace ID, and logs that carry that trace ID.",
    },
    {
        question: "What are the best practices for monitoring microservices?",
        answer:
            "Measure RED (rate, errors, duration) for every service and endpoint and USE (utilization, saturation, errors) for the resources under them. Propagate the W3C traceparent header on every hop, including message queues, and put the trace ID on every log line. Alert on symptoms users notice, such as error rate and SLO burn at entry points, and keep metric labels bounded while sampling traces deliberately.",
    },
    {
        question: "What metrics should I monitor for microservices?",
        answer:
            "Request rate, error rate and latency percentiles (p50, p95, p99) per service and endpoint, which is the RED method. For CPU, memory, connection pools and thread pools, utilization, saturation and errors, which is the USE method. For each queue, consumer lag and the age of the oldest message.",
    },
    {
        question: "What tools are used for microservices monitoring?",
        answer:
            "Most setups pair OpenTelemetry for instrumentation with one or more backends: Prometheus for metrics, Jaeger, Zipkin or Grafana Tempo for traces, Grafana Loki or OpenSearch for logs, or a hosted platform that stores all three. Because OpenTelemetry exports the same OTLP data to any compatible backend, you can choose the backend separately from the instrumentation.",
    },
    {
        question: "How do you trace requests across microservices?",
        answer:
            "Instrument every service with an OpenTelemetry SDK. The caller sends the W3C traceparent header with the trace ID and its own span ID, and the callee starts its span as a child of that span. HTTP and gRPC instrumentation does both for you. For a message queue without instrumentation, inject the trace context into the message headers when you publish and extract it before you start the consumer span.",
    },
    {
        question: "Should I use head sampling or tail sampling?",
        answer:
            "Head sampling decides when a trace starts and costs almost nothing, but it drops errors and slow requests at the same rate as everything else. Tail sampling decides after the trace has finished, so it can keep every error and every slow trace, but it needs an OpenTelemetry Collector that holds whole traces in memory. Derive request metrics from spans before either sampler drops anything.",
    },
];

